From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 11:09:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] plugin: realize a pod's links with a bounded number in flight

Every link of a pod was realized strictly one after another
(plugin/meshnet.go, the `for _, link := range links` loop). Each iteration
blocks on IO the others do not share: a meshnetClient.Get RPC to the local
daemon, koko.EnsureVethWire (two netns entries plus link creation) or
koko.MakeVxLan, a remote gRPC dial and Update to the PEER NODE's daemon, and
two SetTxChecksumOff netns entries. That latency is paid once per link,
inside one 90 s ADD budget, for every pod on a KNE bringup.

The loop body becomes wireLink(), lifted verbatim except that its control
flow now travels in a linkOutcome value instead of `continue`, `return err`
and appends to two shared slices -- concurrency is impossible while a loop
body reaches out and mutates createdLinks and linkErrs. runLinks() then runs
them with at most N in flight and returns the outcomes IN LINK ORDER, so
rollback order and the "realized N of M links" message stay deterministic
however completion interleaves.

WHY THIS IS SAFE, precisely:
  * No shared mutable state is left in the per-link path. Each call returns
    its own outcome; the only package-level variable it reads
    (interNodeLinkType) is set once before cmdAdd runs.
  * Every namespace entry goes through containernetworking ns.Do, which runs
    its callback on its OWN goroutine with runtime.LockOSThread and restores
    (or abandons) that thread afterwards. It is goroutine-safe by
    construction; the init()-time LockOSThread is about the MAIN thread and
    is untouched.
  * gRPC ClientConns are safe for concurrent use.
  * The two koko creation paths serialize themselves exactly where they must
    and nowhere else: EnsureVethWire locks both wire ENDPOINT slots (koko
    0006) and MakeVxLan locks its target slot plus its host VNI tuple (koko
    0010). Two links of one pod always have different local ifnames and
    different deterministic VNIs (0006), so they never contend. No lock's
    scope is widened by this patch; the concurrency is safe because the locks
    were already the right shape.

Bounded, not unbounded: a pod with 40 links must not open 40 concurrent gRPC
dials to one peer node's daemon. Default 4, `linkConcurrency` in the CNI conf
or MESHNET_LINK_CONCURRENCY in the environment; 1 restores exactly the old
serial behaviour and is the kill switch.

"Abort at the first fatal error" is preserved by cancelling the link-phase
context on the first fatal outcome, so siblings stop at their next RPC
instead of running to completion for a pod that is already lost. Their
outcomes are still collected, so anything they DID create is still rolled
back.

ONE BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, forced by the restructure and worth having: the
peer-not-alive branch ended in `if err != nil || !ok.Response { return err }`,
where err is NIL when the trigger was !ok.Response. In the old loop that
returned nil from cmdAdd without printing a CNI result, which multus reports
as "failed to unmarshal raw result: unexpected end of JSON input" -- a
message naming nothing. A nil fatal has no meaning in an outcome value, so
the error is now synthesized, exactly as 0009 already did at the sibling park
site twelve lines above.

Why upstream wants this: nothing about the serialization is deliberate --
it is just what a for loop does -- and the locking that makes overlapping
safe is already in koko. It is a straight latency win on the hot path with a
one-value kill switch.

Positive controls: TestRunLinksBoundsConcurrency proves links overlap AT ALL
(peak must REACH the limit, which is 1 under the old loop) and that nothing
starts beyond it; plus order preservation, the limit-1 serial escape hatch,
fatal-cancels-the-rest, and that a sibling's created link survives a
concurrent fatal. The whole plugin suite passes under -race.

Executed negative controls, each of which made the suite red:
  * forcing the semaphore to depth 1 -> "only 1 of 4 links started; the
    phase is not running them concurrently"
  * removing the bound (semaphore = len(links)) -> "8 links started beyond
    the limit of 4: the bound is not enforced"
  * dropping the cancel on a fatal -> "0 of 3 sibling links saw the
    cancellation"
